title: Mass writes in the store
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A write to many records files one event per operation in the field's list instead of an entry per record; reads replay the pending events, a fold writes them in. The rows and the log are still written record by record.

Setting a tag on ten thousand files (`mf … field set|add|remove` on a query,
[[Query endpoints]]) is one write to many records. Indexing it record by record has
two costs. Filing each record under the value it gains is cheap — the holders of one
value are neighbours in the store. Taking each record ''out'' of the value it loses is
not: that value has to be known, so every record is read first.

!! One event per operation

A field has a list of ''pending events'' (table `events`, keyed by field and a
number). A write to many records appends one, naming the operation and the records
— a bitmap of dense ids — and files nothing per record:

|!Event |!A value's holders become |
|add `v` to `B` |`∪ B`, for `v` |
|remove `v` from `B` |`∖ B`, for `v` |
|replace by `vals` on `B` |`∖ B`, then `∪ B` for the values of `vals` |

A replacement never names the values it removes. The holders of a value are what is
''stored'' — its run of the value partition or its posting, and for a `ref` its
referrers ([[Store tables]]) — with the field's events replayed over it, in order.

What cannot wait is kept exact at once, by set operations whatever the number of
records: the count of values of each record (an increment on a set of ids is a carry
from bit to bit, [[Store tables]]), the `absent` and long-text sets, the trigrams
([[Text search in the store]]: a new text's entries by value, or the ids pending by
metarecord), and the histograms for a value added or removed.

!! What reads do

* ''Equality, referrers, a text search by value'': the value's stored holders, replayed.
* ''A range, a `!=`, a scan'': the values an event names are left out of the scan of what is stored and read one by one, replayed; every other value has only lost the records a replacement was written on, which are subtracted once.
* ''A sort by walking the values'': not while the field has events pending — the page is fetched from the rows ([[Sorting and postings]]). Merging the named values into the walk is not built.

A field without pending events — nearly all of them, nearly always — is read as
before, for one read of its (empty) list.

!! Order

While a field has events pending, a write to ''one'' record of it is an event too, of
one id: written into what is stored, it would be overwritten by the replay of an older
event. The counts and the sets above are kept at each write, in order, and never
replayed.

!! The fold

`KvStore::fold` — and a reindex, and a write that leaves a field more than 64 events
(`EVENTS_MAX`, to be measured) — writes the events into what is stored, oldest first,
and empties the list. A replacement is where its removed values are found: the
field's stored values are walked for the records it was written on, and the
histograms give back the rows they held. Until then the histogram of a field with
events counts those rows, so `mf repo check` does not hold it to the rows; the
partition and the referrers are held to them as replayed
([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).

!! What is not there yet

The ''rows'' of each record and the ''log'' are still written record by record: a
mass write reads and writes every record it changes, as before. Filing the rows by
part of the id space and the log as bitmaps are the next steps of the storage
redesign; this one is their footing, and saves nothing by itself.

Positions (`tree_ref`, `hierarchy_ref`) are never part of an event: each is written
on its own ([[One position per forest]]). Nor is a `Nothing` or a text over 512 bytes
added or removed.

`tests/kv_events.rs` holds every reader to the oracle with events pending, after a
fold and after a reindex.
